    {
      "entryId": "ramcharitmanas-bala-kanda-0186",
      "sequence": 198,
      "meaning": "Brahmā’s hymn hails the Lord of gods, giver of joy, protector of the surrendered, friend of cows and Brahmins, enemy of demons, and beloved husband of the Ocean’s daughter. No one knows the mystery of his wondrous protection of gods and earth, yet the naturally compassionate friend of the helpless will show grace. Imperishable, indwelling, all-pervading bliss, unknowable and beyond the senses, he is liberated from māyā and his deeds are pure. Dispassionate, undeluded sages lovingly meditate on him day and night. He created the threefold cosmos without a second helper; the gods, who know neither devotion nor worship, ask him to heed their distress. He destroys worldly fear, delights sages and crushes calamity; abandoning every other clever recourse in mind, word and act, the gods seek refuge. Sarasvatī, Vedas, Śeṣa and countless sages cannot know him, yet the Vedas proclaim his love for the lowly. This beautiful repository of virtues, churning-mountain for the ocean of becoming and mass of joy, is the Lord at whose lotus feet all frightened gods and siddhas bow.",
